{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates whether either lottery contains a sure outcome (i.e., an outcome that occurs with 100% probability). In this decision problem, both lotteries have probabilistic outcomes only: Lottery A offers 3000 Euros with 0.2% probability and 0 Euros with 99.8%, while Lottery B offers 6000 Euros with 0.1% and 0 Euros with 99.9%. Neither lottery has a sure outcome (100% probability outcome). Therefore, the condition 'presence of a sure outcome' is absent in both lotteries. The decision reason prefers the lottery *without* a sure outcome. Since neither lottery has a sure outcome, the reason would be indifferent or possibly favor both equally—though strictly speaking, the condition it acts upon (presence of a sure outcome) does not apply to either. Thus, the reason cannot clearly prefer one lottery over the other based on its rule.",
